tom@soda.berkeley.edu (Tom Holub) comments on multi-player nethack:

>This is a great idea except for one thing: it's stupid.  You have three
>choices: either make every player in the game take their turn before
>anyone can move, or have a ticker like in a MUD, or allow anyone to 
>move at any time (as you can now).  The first would slow the game
>to a crawl (but keep the game reasonably similar), the second would
>turn it into a pretty lame MUD, and the third would turn it into a 
>really lame version of Hunt.
>
>How do you stop a level 20 character with a +8 Stormbringer from sitting
>around the level 1 steps waiting for new characters and killing them
>in one blow?  Great tactic, he'd get all kinds of healing potions, 
>a tinning kit, the occasional magic marker, rings and wands, etc.  
>
>Also, do you have just one dungeon?  Wouldn't that get boring once you
>knew it?  If each dungeon is random, how do the players ever interact?
>And don't even *think* about being able to find food in a dungeon
>with 10 or 20 players.

I agree that nethack is designed to be a single player game, but there
are some reasonable ways to incorporate multi player features.

How about a special level (or part of a level) that's a dimensional
nexus?  It could be clearly marked, placed fairly deep in the dungeon,
and avoided by those who didn't want to deal with the outside world.
It could, for example, take the form of a transplanar inn where
characters can meet and exchange information and items.  Since conflict
is inevitable, create an "arena pit" run by the innkeeper where
testosterone crazed barbarians can do battle (and where other characters
at the Inn can wager on the result).  There could also be a general
store for buying and selling items, a message board, a physician,
gambling...  Any number of interesting features are possible.

This solves many of the problems that Berkeley boy has brought up.
And of course those who don't like it could simply not compile it
into their game.

